import{_ as n,P as i,d as a,e as o,y as r,q as s,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const b=JSON.parse(`{"title":"A.4 HV04 Generative Node (Full Interface Card)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"A.4 HV04 Generative Node (Full Interface Card)","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1144,"xkLessons":{"A.4":{"title":"A.4 HV04 Generative Node (Complete Interface Card)","lead":"HV04 separates generative conditions, actors, and events. An unidentified actor does not mean an absence of actors has been proved; the fact of generation creates neither justification, complete responsibility, nor continuing authority.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 3852–3917; original site location /theory/appendix-a/a-04#section-a-4. For the current full text, see the theory-v2 source; historical research manuscripts and excerpts remain available for checking in their original downloadable versions.","Brief quotation, translated from Chinese: “Generation must be separated into generative conditions GC, generative subjects GS, and generative events GE.” GC concerns how conditions and constraints permit new states to form; GS denotes acting subjects with corresponding capabilities, roles, and standing; GE denotes detectable new states or transitions. The three may be connected but cannot replace one another. An event occurring does not mean an actor has been identified, and a visible person does not mean that person created the entire result. [T-main; T-types]","HV04-R0-generation-typing permits separately registering candidate GC/GS/GE and unidentified-actor states, with conclusions stopping at strong typing. No registered inferential dependencies does not mean evidence is unnecessary: EVIDENCE, SOURCE, and specific object material remain required. Successful classification also does not establish a causal generation mechanism among the three. [T-main]","The original card permits emergent generation without an identifiable actor, but “currently unidentified” is not “proved to have no actor.” Observed local interactions and overall changes can be objects of bridging research. A claim about an actorless mechanism additionally requires corresponding channel and comparison evidence; an unknown initiator cannot be concealed behind “natural emergence.” [T-main]","HV04-R1-generation-mechanism requires a preregistered G2-instance identifying an above-threshold effect of GC, GS, or an actorless emergent channel on a specified GE state transition, plus CAUSAL and E4. What is permitted is a candidate generation mechanism and its typing within specified scales, windows, and channels. It does not automatically establish that any technology has intentions, that any crisis wants to change institutions, or that the most visible person bears all responsibility. [T-main; T-G2]","Register nine-axis scales and times for GC, GS, and GE separately: condition accumulation, actor action, event timing, and diffusion delays may differ. Generative subjects, conditions, and events may change with scale. Moving across levels requires new units and populations, representation relations, responsibility types, and external impacts; individual initiation cannot directly establish the intention of an organization as a whole. [T-main]","The fact of generation establishes neither justification nor complete responsibility; having initiated something does not grant an actor permanent continuing authorization. Outputs may establish uptake needs, descriptions of state transitions, and a starting point for a responsibility chain, but a “starting point” is not a final attribution of responsibility. Starting, spreading, or stopping any real-world process requires independent choice and authorization; the interface does not issue orders itself. [T-main]","Those classified as actors may safely challenge attributed intentions, roles, and authorization, with anti-retaliation protection, and request review outside the original typing chain. When rollback is authorized, correct GC/GS/GE types, actually remove erroneous attributions of intention/responsibility and downstream effects, and preserve versions and completion verification. Merely renaming “responsible person” as “generator” while continuing to affect evaluations is insufficient. [T-main]","The SEC's 2013 order on the 2012 Knight incident separately records omissions in code deployment, activation of an old program, and order outputs. This can help record technical conditions, personnel conduct, and event outcomes separately. The technical chain does not make a program an intentional actor, and the order's regulatory findings cannot be substituted in this explanation for a complete responsibility judgment about any individual. [P-KNIGHT]","Field-by-field study of all 39 fields (“Original card” below retains the field’s complete content in translation; “Interpretation” specifies its role and irreplaceable responsibility).","01 | Interface ID (id). Original card, translated from Chinese: HV04. Interpretation: This is the short identifier within the card collection, tracking the same interface; it does not mean a real object has been proved. [T-main]","02 | Qualified ID (qualified_id). Original card, translated from Chinese: human_variable:HV04. Interpretation: A qualified namespace avoids name collisions; references to this ID must still carry the object and evidence status. [T-main]","03 | Name (name). Original card, translated from Chinese: Generative node. Interpretation: The name provides an entry point for reading, not an automatic label for a particular person, group, or real structure. [T-main]","04 | Claim type (claim_type). Original card: H. Interpretation: H identifies the role of a human-instance/interface proposition; it cannot be upgraded into formal proof or an unconditional law of the world. [T-main]","05 | Contract role (contract_role). Original card: human_variable_interface. Interpretation: this variable-interface contract specifies how to enter analysis; a field’s existence does not mean an empirical instance has been established. [T-main]","06 | Proposition (proposition). Original card, translated from Chinese: Generation must be separated into generative conditions GC, generative subjects GS, and generative events GE, permitting emergent generation without an identifiable actor. Interpretation: GC/GS/GE are distinct strong types. Emergence without an identifiable actor is permitted, but an unknown-actor state cannot directly be upgraded to proved absence of an actor. [T-main]","07 | Scope (scope). Original card, translated from Chinese: Formation of new actions, organizations, institutions, or state transitions in human structures. Interpretation: The scope lists task domains that may be examined; it does not mean every object within them has empirical support. [T-main]","08 | Pause condition (pause_condition). Original card, translated from Chinese: When conditions are personified, events treated as actors, or actor standing not established on grounds, pause the corresponding actor or typing assertions; R0 may still separately register candidate GC, GS, GE, and unidentified-actor states, without treating unidentified as proved actorless. Interpretation: When personification is erroneous or actor standing insufficient, pause the corresponding assertions. R0's candidate conditions, actors, events, and unidentified states may still remain separately. [T-main]","09 | Inferential dependencies (inferential_requires). Original card, translated from Chinese: None (empty set). Interpretation: This is explicitly an empty set, with no inferential dependencies registered. It does not waive SOURCE/EVIDENCE protocols or turn R0 typing into causal proof. [T-main]","10 | Protocol dependencies (protocol_requires). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. EVIDENCE; 2. SOURCE. Interpretation: Protocol dependencies constrain evidence collection and expression; completing protocols does not independently establish the truth of empirical propositions. [T-main]","11 | Qualification / specialization (specializes). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: an empty set means only that no such specialization relations are registered, not that evidential responsibility is absent; a nonempty set also does not imply universal validity. [T-main]","12 | Applicable-object references (applies_to). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: applicable-object references cannot replace the current object contract; an empty set is explicit registration, not permission to apply to every object. [T-main]","13 | Conditional-support routes (conditional_support_routes). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. route_id=HV04-R0-generation-typing;; claim_level=descriptive_classification;; when=The required GC, GS, and GE fields can be registered separately, while preserving actorless emergence and unidentified-actor states.;; additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set);; additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set);; allowed_conclusion=Separately register candidate generative conditions, candidate generative subjects, and candidate generative events, without substituting them for one another.;; result_ceiling=Strong typing only; the existence of any condition, actor, or event does not establish the other two or a causal generation mechanism.; 2. route_id=HV04-R1-generation-mechanism;; claim_level=mechanism_explanation;; when=A preregistered G2-instance identifies an above-threshold effect of GC, GS, or an actorless emergent channel on a specified GE state transition.;; additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance;; additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;; allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate generation mechanism within the specified scale, window, and channel and its GC, GS, GE typing.;; result_ceiling=Do not personify conditions, infer actors backward from events, or derive justification, responsibility, and authorization from the fact of generation. Interpretation: R0 only classifies. R1 requires preregistered G2 identification of GC, GS, or an actorless channel's effect on specified GE, plus CAUSAL/E4; an event having occurred does not justify inferring an actor backward. [T-main]","14 | Allowed inference (allowed_inference). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Conditional generative paths; 2. Generation with or without an actor. Interpretation: These are permitted directions of conclusion; which is actually reached, and at what strength, remains bounded by the selected route and material. [T-main]","15 | Prohibited leap (prohibited_leap). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Technologies or crises have intentions; 2. Generative subjects automatically possess continuing authorization. Interpretation: These are common boundary crossings that cannot be avoided merely by rephrasing; examine actual inferences and subsequent use, not just wording. [T-main]","16 | Nine-axis scale profile (scale_profile). Original card, translated from Chinese: SP=<A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J>; A=Aggregation level: units of condition exposure, actor action, and generative events, each with its own case population, distribution, and aggregation rules; X=Spatial scope: generation sites, organizational or platform boundaries, diffusion regions, and cross-domain environments; T=Temporal span: condition-accumulation periods, actor-action windows, event times, and diffusion delays; O=Organizational level: action roles, generative teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies; C=Causal level: generative events, actor–condition interaction mechanisms, meso-level generative structures, institutional arrangements, and system conditions; R=Observation resolution: raw conditions, actions, and events; generation sequences, cases, outcome distributions, transition indicators, and summaries, with compression losses registered; I=Range of impact: direct participants in generation, those taking it up, indirectly affected people, second-order consequences, and cross-domain and intergenerational impacts; N=Network-topology scope: condition propagation, actor collaboration, event diffusion, and emergent connections; J=Jurisdiction and authorization scope: separately register authorization for identifying generation, initiating changes, investing resources, and handling diffusion; GC, GS, and GE each register their own scale. Interpretation: In addition to checking all nine axes separately, GC conditions, GS actors, and GE events each need their own scales; one overall scale must not conceal cross-level differences among conditions, actions, and consequences. [T-main]","17 | Effective object (effective_object). Original card, translated from Chinese: A combination of conditions, actors, and events forming a detectable state transition. Interpretation: This describes the object corresponding to the interface; choosing a low-threshold descriptive route does not permit treating functional meaning in the name as proven causality. [T-main]","18 | Cross-scale invariants (scale_invariants). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Strong-type separation of GC, GS, and GE. Interpretation: Invariants are comparative responsibilities to preserve during transfer, not a claim that variable values are unchanged at every scale. [T-main]","19 | Required additions for elevation (required_scale_additions). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. New units and populations; 2. Representation relations; 3. Responsibility types; 4. External impacts. Interpretation: Elevation is not copying and enlarging the original table. When material about new populations, interfaces, responsibility positions, and other items is incomplete, limit conclusions that depend on them. [T-main]","20 | Semantics changing with scale (changing_semantics). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Generative subjects, conditions, and events may change with scale. Interpretation: A word may change meaning when the object or scale changes; rebuild the correspondence rather than assume the original meaning applies unchanged. [T-main]","21 | Non-applicable objects (non_applicable_objects). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Stable descriptions with no formation of a new state or claim about generation. Interpretation: Non-applicability constraints prevent unconditional projection of interfaces concerning human norms, responsibility, and experience; they do not deny that other theories may study these objects. [T-main]","22 | Forbidden elevation (forbidden_elevation). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Elevating conditions or events into intentional actors. Interpretation: Inferences from local parts to wholes need separate proof; broad impact, grand names, and formal similarity cannot supply representativeness or authorization. [T-main]","23 | State set (state). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Latent; 2. Triggered; 3. Formed; 4. Interrupted; 5. Diffusing. Interpretation: States are descriptive classifications requiring evidence, not maturity ladders for scoring people; state changes require windows, grounds, and versions. [T-main]","24 | Observables (observables). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Time records of candidate conditions appearing, changing, or being removed; 2. Generative subjects' capabilities, authorization, decisions, and actual actions; 3. Preregistered state transitions before and after generative events; 4. Bridging records between local interactions and overall outcomes in actorless emergence. Interpretation: Observable clues indicate where to find material; a single clue's existence is not proof of a complete mechanism or causal effect. [T-main]","25 | Evidence requirements (evidence). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Initiation records; 2. Condition windows; 3. Actor actions; 4. Actorless interaction mechanisms. Interpretation: Gather listed evidence according to route and task relevance. Distinguish unknown, not collected, inapplicable, and unavailable under protection; do not compel disclosure merely to fill the table. [T-main]","26 | Input dependencies and interface content (input_dependencies). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Orientation anchors; 2. Resource and institutional conditions; 3. Causal contracts. Interpretation: This states what enters from upstream and with what qualifications; gaps must not quietly disappear during transfer between interfaces. [T-main]","27 | Output effects and variable flows (output_effects). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. Uptake needs; 2. State transitions; 3. Starting points of responsibility chains. Interpretation: Variable flows indicate which downstream judgments may depend on this interface; passing references does not guarantee downstream conclusions, actual repair, or authorization. [T-main]","28 | Time window and lag (time_window_and_lag). Original card: Register accumulated conditions, triggering events, and formation lags. Interpretation: Occurrence, observation, execution, and the arrival of effects may happen at different times; short-term material cannot automatically certify extension beyond the window. [T-main]","29 | Uncertainty (uncertainty). Original card: Record joint generation, emergence without an agent, and unidentifiable agents. Interpretation: Locate uncertainty in specific material and positions; do not merely write an overall confidence score that conceals positions never covered at all. [T-main]","30 | Local exclusion zone (local_exclusion_zone). Original card: Omitted informal initiators and affected positions. Interpretation: An exclusion zone limits the coverage of the current inference; it does not erase these people or facts from the world. Material unavailable for safety reasons is neither consent nor evidence of no problem. [T-main]","31 | Affected positions (affected_positions). Original card: 1. Initiators; 2. Those taking up the process; 3. Beneficiaries; 4. Affected people. Interpretation: Register both directly and indirectly affected people; visible members do not represent all cost bearers. [T-main]","32 | Uptake carrier (carrier). Original card: 1. Initiators; 2. Procedures; 3. Technical facilities; 4. Relational networks. Interpretation: A carrier is the structure or process that actually carries something. Its name, completed construction, and delivery of the required function require different evidence. [T-main]","33 | Responsible party (responsible_subject). Original card: 1. Actual actors; 2. Decision-makers; 3. Authorizers. Interpretation: Specify who proposes a judgment and who operates or uses it; registering responsibility does not automatically constitute adjudication of legal liability or new authorization. [T-main]","34 | Normative status (normative_status). Original card: Facts of generation do not establish normative justification or completeness of responsibility. Interpretation: Distinguish descriptive, normative, and bridging status as specified by the card. Established facts do not independently determine value rankings, and normative reasons do not automatically establish empirical effects. [T-main]","35 | Judgment ceiling (judgment_ceiling). Original card: Up to the explanatory level when the mechanism chain is complete. Interpretation: A complete mechanism can support the explanatory level; R0 still reaches only candidate classification, and R1 likewise concerns a candidate mechanism for a specified channel. Neither grants responsibility assignments or continuing authorization. [T-main]","36 | Action ceiling (action_ceiling). Original card: This variable generates only candidate classifications of GC, GS, and GE, descriptions of state transitions, and requirements for additional evidence. It does not authorize initiating, spreading, or stopping generative processes; any real-world adjustment must separately pass C12, an explicit runtime N premise, J authorization, and O procedures. Interpretation: The interface itself does not confer real-world authority to act; actual actions require separate checks of choice, C12, explicit N, valid J, O, protection, and validity periods. [T-main]","37 | Counterexamples (counterexamples). Original card: 1. Technical conditions are incorrectly described as a goal-directed generative subject; 2. An emergent process without a unified initiator is forcibly attributed to one visible person. Interpretation: Use counterexamples to limit unconditional extrapolation. They may require narrowing the scope or obtaining new evidence, but do not automatically invalidate all bounded uses. [T-main]","38 | Appeal (appeal). Original card: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, those classified as generative subjects may challenge attributions of intent, role, and authorization through safe, accessible channels protecting against retaliation, and trigger review independent of the original classification or decision chain. Interpretation: Appeals must be safely accessible, protected against retaliation, and capable of triggering independent review; a name alone does not imply authority to change errors. [T-main]","39 | Rollback (rollback). Original card: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, when rollback is authorized, the designated responsible person corrects GC, GS, and GE types within the specified time limit, actually removes erroneous attributions of intent or responsibility and their downstream effects, and retains versions and verification of completion. Interpretation: Within valid authorization, actually revoke erroneous effects, correct downstream consequences, and verify completion; changing words in a document does not restore rights and resources to the person concerned. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Starting from a claim about a new state, register GC, GS, GE, and their sources separately.","R0 retains candidate status and unknown agents without personifying conditions.","If seeking a mechanism explanation, preregister the specified state transition and G2 channel comparison.","Check the scale, time window, and cross-level bridging for GC, GS, and GE separately.","Effects, normative justification, responsibility, and authorization must each be established; a generator does not automatically retain authority forever.","Disputed attributions may undergo independent review; after authorized correction, remove downstream effects."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"GC: generative conditions","leftText":"Resource, technical, institutional, or situational conditions.","rightTitle":"GS: generative subjects","rightText":"Agents' capabilities, roles, and actions require separate checking."},{"leftTitle":"GE: generative events","leftText":"Detectable state transitions.","rightTitle":"Agent intention","rightText":"Known intent cannot be inferred backward from an outcome."},{"leftTitle":"Unidentified agent","leftText":"Currently unknown or unconfirmed.","rightTitle":"Emergence without an agent","rightText":"Requires mechanism material linking local interactions to an overall result."},{"leftTitle":"Fact of initiation","leftText":"An action was performed.","rightTitle":"Continuing authorization","rightText":"Its specified scope and validity period must be established independently."}],"boundaries":["Original-card counterexample: describing a technical condition as a goal-directed agent.","Original-card counterexample: forcibly attributing a process without a unified initiator to a visible person.","Teaching counterexample: someone speaks publicly first and is then recorded as the sole generator of every subsequent change.","Source boundary: this interface does not authorize initiating, spreading, or stopping generative processes."],"questions":["Can a crisis be described as intentional because it triggers change?","Does failure to find an initiator establish generation without an agent?","Does the existence of GC prove that GE will occur?","Is changing the type label alone enough for correction?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-4/figures/hv04-types.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Conditions, actions, and events in Knight's technical chain","href":"https://www.sec.gov/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Conditions, actions, and events in Knight's technical chain","text":"Sections 15–16 of the SEC's 2013 order record that one server was not updated in the 2012 deployment and that old code was triggered; §§19–21 record problems with messages and output controls.","sourceLabel":"SEC：Knight Capital Americas LLC，Release70694","sourceUrl":"https://www.sec.gov/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","sourceLocator":"Order of 2013-10-16, §II, §§15–16 and 19–21, printed pages 1 and 6–7","boundary":"The regulatory order's bounded findings can serve as a classification comparator; they do not confer intent on a program, directly establish individual responsibility, or certify G2 under this framework.","href":"https://www.sec.gov/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"A.4","start_line":3943,"end_line":4010,"startLine":3943,"endLine":4010}],"outputId":"section-a-4","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: three kinds of object within one new function","text":"A fictional platform opens a new interface, team A publishes a new service under existing permissions, and users form new exchange activities. The interface capability may be listed as GC, team A as a GS candidate, its actual publishing action as evidence of agent action, and a specified change in exchange state as a GE candidate. If users later combine spontaneously to form another state, not all subsequent developments can be attributed to A's original intent; a new object window and mechanism material are needed.","assumptions":"The platform, team, and events are constructed; no actual launch is involved.","boundary":"Candidate classification does not replace G2 or establish A's responsibility for, or authority over, all subsequent behavior."},{"title":"Teaching construction: not knowing who does not mean there was no one","text":"Constructed records show that queue rules changed at a particular time, but the initiation log is missing. Register a candidate event and an unknown agent, rather than ‘emergence without an agent has been proved.’ If a recovered log later shows that an authorized role changed the configuration, update the corresponding GS and version. If a mechanism of distributed interactions is found, its bridging and causal evidence also require separate checks.","assumptions":"A fictional record-recovery scenario.","boundary":"Retaining an unknown presupposes neither a hidden operator nor the absence of an agent; both assertions require evidence."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-card counterexample: describing a technical condition as a goal-directed agent.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-card counterexample: forcibly attributing a process without a unified initiator to a visible person.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: someone speaks publicly first and is then recorded as the sole generator of every subsequent change.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: this interface does not authorize initiating, spreading, or stopping generative processes.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can a crisis be described as intentional because it triggers change?","answer":"No; it may be a condition or an event, while agent intent requires separate checking.","basis":"Prohibited leap."},{"question":"Does failure to find an initiator establish generation without an agent?","answer":"No; distinguish an unidentified agent from a supported mechanism without an agent.","basis":"Pause conditions and R0/R1."},{"question":"Does the existence of GC prove that GE will occur?","answer":"No; the existence of one R0 type does not prove the other two or a generative mechanism.","basis":"R0 ceiling."},{"question":"Is changing the type label alone enough for correction?","answer":"Erroneous attributions of intent or responsibility and their downstream effects must be revoked, and completion verified.","basis":"rollback。"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-4/figures/hv04-types.png","alt":"Register GC conditions, GS agents, and GE events separately; an unidentified agent is not proof of the absence of an agent.","caption":"Based on the strong types and pause conditions in A.4."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-4/figures/hv04-routes.png","alt":"R0: candidate classification; R1: preregistered G2 effects on specified state transitions; facts of generation do not confer normative justification, responsibility, or authorization.","caption":"Based on the two complete routes in A.4."}],"diagramBrief":"The first diagram separates GC/GS/GE; the second distinguishes classification and mechanism routes. A blank agent remains unknown; arrows do not create intent or responsibility.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["There is no actual G2-instance; this topic explains only classification and eligibility for mechanism claims.","Unidentified agents, emergence without an agent, joint generation, and legal liability all require proportionate independent evidence; visibility does not determine them."],"taskId":"S-A-4","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.4, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 3766–3831","claim_support":["All 39 fields of HV04 and its generation-classification/mechanism routes."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-types","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§7.2–7.2.3, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 1486–1501","claim_support":["Definitions of the strong types GC, GS, and GE and the prohibition on mixing them."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-G2","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.4, Chinese source lines 610–619","claim_support":["Specified channel effects and dimension-by-dimension boundedness."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-KNIGHT","title":"SEC：Knight Capital Americas LLC，Release70694","url":"https://www.sec.gov/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2013-10-16","locator":"Order of 2013-10-16, §II, §§15–16 and 19–21, printed pages 1 and 6–7","claim_support":["The specific technical chain involving a deployment omission, triggering of old code, and output controls; the document contains findings in a settlement order."],"limits":["The author read the order locally in part; except as to jurisdiction, the respondent neither admitted nor denied the findings. The technical chain is not used to confer intent on machines, automatically assign individual responsibility, or masquerade as a G2 instance."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"A.4 HV04 Generative nodes (complete interface card); Chinese source lines 3852–3917; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-4/source/S-A-4.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"816621aa3602f329e2d7ad9bae61070d95be77448e1829141f9038114b31844a","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-04.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-04.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-a/a-04.md"};function l(u,e,p,h,g,f){const t=i("SectionLesson");return a(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=r('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-a-4" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">A.4</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV04 Generative Node (Full Interface Card) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-a-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A.4 HV04 Generative Node (Full Interface Card) {#section-a-4}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A. Identity, Proposition, and Scope</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interface ID (<code>id</code>)</td><td>HV04</td></tr><tr><td>Qualified ID (<code>qualified_id</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable:HV04</code></td></tr><tr><td>Name (<code>name</code>)</td><td>Generative Node</td></tr><tr><td>Claim type (<code>claim_type</code>)</td><td>H</td></tr><tr><td>Contract role (<code>contract_role</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable_interface</code></td></tr><tr><td>Proposition (<code>proposition</code>)</td><td>Generation must be separated into generative condition GC, generative subject GS, and generative event GE. Emergent generation can occur without an identifiable subject.</td></tr><tr><td>Scope (<code>scope</code>)</td><td>Formation of new actions, organizations, institutions, or state transitions in human structures</td></tr><tr><td>Conditions for suspension (<code>pause_condition</code>)</td><td>Suspend the relevant claims about the subject or the type when a condition is personified, an event is treated as a subject, or an entity is classified as a generative subject without grounds. R0 may still register candidate GC, GS, and GE and a status of unidentified subject separately. Failure to identify a subject does not establish that there is none.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>B. Formal Dependencies and Limits on Conclusions</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Inferential dependencies (<code>inferential_requires</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol dependencies (<code>protocol_requires</code>)</td><td>1. EVIDENCE<br>2. SOURCE</td></tr><tr><td>Qualification/specialization (<code>specializes</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>References to applicable objects (<code>applies_to</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional support routes (<code>conditional_support_routes</code>)</td><td>1. route_id=HV04-R0-generation-typing;<br>claim_level=descriptive_classification;<br>when=The required fields for GC, GS, and GE can be registered separately, and emergence without a subject and the status of an unidentified subject are retained;<br>additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set);<br>additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set);<br>allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate generative condition, candidate generative subject, and candidate generative event separately. None may substitute for another;<br>result_ceiling=Classification by strong type only. The existence of a condition, subject, or event does not establish either of the other two or a causal mechanism of generation.<br>2. route_id=HV04-R1-generation-mechanism;<br>claim_level=mechanism_explanation;<br>when=A pre-registered G2-instance identifies an above-threshold effect of GC, GS, or a channel of emergence without a subject on the specified GE state transition;<br>additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate mechanism of generation and its GC, GS, and GE classifications for the specified scale, window, and channel;<br>result_ceiling=Do not personify conditions, infer a subject backward from an event, or infer normative justification, responsibility, or authorization from the fact of generation.</td></tr><tr><td>Permitted inferences (<code>allowed_inference</code>)</td><td>1. Conditional paths of generation. 2. Generation with or without a subject</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited leaps (<code>prohibited_leap</code>)</td><td>1. Attributing intent to a technology or a crisis<br>2. Assuming a generative subject automatically has continuing authorization</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>C. Nine-Axis Scale and Object Contract</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nine-axis scale profile (<code>scale_profile</code>)</td><td>SP=&lt;A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J&gt;. A=Aggregation level covers units of exposure to conditions, action by subjects, and generative events, with their respective populations of cases, distributions, and aggregation rules. X=Spatial extent covers sites of generation, organizational or platform boundaries, areas of diffusion, and cross-domain environments. T=Time span covers the period of accumulation of conditions, the window of the subject’s action, the time of the event, and diffusion lag. O=Organizational level covers roles in action, generative teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies. C=Causal level covers generative events, mechanisms of interaction between subjects and conditions, meso-level structures of generation, institutional arrangements, and systemic conditions. R=Resolution covers the original conditions, actions, and events, sequences of generation, individual cases, outcome distributions, transition metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. I=Scope of influence covers direct participants in generation, people taking up the work, indirectly affected people, second-order consequences, and impacts across domains and generations. N=Network topology scope covers propagation of conditions, collaboration among subjects, diffusion of events, and emergent connections. J=Scope of jurisdiction and authorization requires separate authorization records for identification of generation, initiation of change, commitment of resources, and action concerning diffusion. Register scales separately for GC, GS, and GE</td></tr><tr><td>Effective object (<code>effective_object</code>)</td><td>A combination of conditions, subjects, and events that forms a detectable state transition</td></tr><tr><td>Scale invariants (<code>scale_invariants</code>)</td><td>1. Separation of GC, GS, and GE as strong types</td></tr><tr><td>Required additions for scale elevation (<code>required_scale_additions</code>)</td><td>1. New units and populations<br>2. Relations of representation<br>3. Kinds of responsibility<br>4. External impacts</td></tr><tr><td>What changes with scale (<code>changing_semantics</code>)</td><td>1. Generative subjects, conditions, and events can change with scale</td></tr><tr><td>Inapplicable objects (<code>non_applicable_objects</code>)</td><td>1. Stable descriptions without formation of a new state or a claim about generation</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited elevations (<code>forbidden_elevation</code>)</td><td>1. Elevating a condition or an event to a subject with intent</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>D. State, Evidence, and Variable Flows</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State set (<code>state</code>)</td><td>1. Potential<br>2. Triggered<br>3. Forming<br>4. Interrupted<br>5. Diffusing</td></tr><tr><td>Observables (<code>observables</code>)</td><td>1. Records of when candidate conditions appear, change, or are removed<br>2. The generative subject’s capabilities, authorization, decisions, and actual actions<br>3. The pre-registered state transition from before to after the generative event<br>4. Records of the bridge from local interaction to aggregate outcome in emergence without a subject</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence requirements (<code>evidence</code>)</td><td>1. Records of initiation<br>2. Windows for conditions<br>3. Actions by subjects<br>4. Mechanisms of interaction without a subject</td></tr><tr><td>Input dependencies and interface content (<code>input_dependencies</code>)</td><td>1. Orientation anchor. 2. Resources and institutional conditions. 3. Causal contract</td></tr><tr><td>Output effects and variable flows (<code>output_effects</code>)</td><td>1. Need for uptake. 2. State transitions. 3. The start of a chain of responsibility</td></tr><tr><td>Time window and lag (<code>time_window_and_lag</code>)</td><td>Register accumulation of conditions, triggering events, and lags in formation</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty (<code>uncertainty</code>)</td><td>Record joint generation, emergence without a subject, and subjects that cannot be identified</td></tr><tr><td>Local exclusion zone (<code>local_exclusion_zone</code>)</td><td>Overlooked informal initiators and affected positions</td></tr><tr><td>Affected positions (<code>affected_positions</code>)</td><td>1. Initiators<br>2. People taking up the work<br>3. Beneficiaries<br>4. People affected</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>E. Uptake, Responsibility, Normative Status, Ceilings, and Correction</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Uptake carriers (<code>carrier</code>)</td><td>1. Initiators<br>2. Procedures<br>3. Technical facilities<br>4. Relationship networks</td></tr><tr><td>Responsible parties (<code>responsible_subject</code>)</td><td>1. Those who actually act. 2. Decision makers. 3. Parties granting authorization</td></tr><tr><td>Normative status (<code>normative_status</code>)</td><td>The fact of generation does not establish normative justification or a complete attribution of responsibility</td></tr><tr><td>Judgment ceiling (<code>judgment_ceiling</code>)</td><td>Up to the level of explanation when the mechanism chain is complete</td></tr><tr><td>Action ceiling (<code>action_ceiling</code>)</td><td>This variable produces only candidate classifications for GC, GS, and GE, descriptions of state transitions, and needs for more evidence. It does not authorize starting, spreading, or stopping a generative process. Any real-world adjustment separately requires C12, explicit N premises at runtime, J authorization, and O procedures</td></tr><tr><td>Counterexamples (<code>counterexamples</code>)</td><td>1. A technological condition is incorrectly described as a generative subject with a goal<br>2. An emergent process without a unified initiator is forced into an attribution to one visible person</td></tr><tr><td>Appeal (<code>appeal</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, people classified as generative subjects can challenge attributions of intent, role, and authorization through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection. This triggers review independent of the original classification or decision chain</td></tr><tr><td>Rollback (<code>rollback</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, once rollback is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline correct the GC, GS, and GE types and actually remove erroneous attributions of intent or responsibility and their downstream effects. Preserve versions and verification of completion</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>',1)),s(t,{section:"A.4"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const v=n(d,[["render",l]]);export{b as __pageData,v as default};
